{
  "configuration_id": "config_018",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_016 (multimodal, single-profile, PCA_n2000+ResNet50, concat) at 0.527 acc, which beats RNA-only (0.485) by ~4 pts, confirming that fusion adds value. Concat and weighted fusion are nearly equivalent (0.527 vs 0.523), so fusion method is not the bottleneck. However, the profile-setting axis (single vs. multi) is completely untested, and it is a major structural design choice that could substantially change how much information the model extracts from the cellular data. Question: Does aggregating multiple cell profiles (via avgpool) on top of the already-validated PCA_n2000 + ResNet50 + concat pipeline yield a meaningful gain over the single-profile baseline? Why this is the best next experiment: config_018 is the minimal, most direct extension of the current best—changing only the profile setting to multiprofile with the simpler avgpool aggregation—while holding every other variable constant. This isolates the effect of multiprofile, making the result interpretable. If it improves, we have identified a new productive axis (and can then explore transformer aggregation); if it does not, we confirm single-profile sufficiency and can redirect budget toward the image-encoder or RNA-encoder axes. Under a tight budget, resolving this unexplored binary with a single clean comparison is the highest-information-per-experiment choice."
}

